>
> You probably know all this, but...
>
> In many circumstances, a "message retransmitted" might be considered
> an indication of an underlying hardware problem or misconfiguration.
> Your counters have some of those. In different circumstances with
> different network protocols such retransmissions may not show up at
> the higher layers because they would be transparently retried and
> recovered (although a decent networking implementation would still
> count them for diagnostics purposes). LAT is a lightweight protocol
> with little scope for retries and error recovery; when things go wrong
> at the hardware level problems quickly start becoming visible to users
> and applications. Are there any other signs of packet retransmission
> anywhere on this box?
>
> Thinwire ethernet in particular used to be famous for working with
> DECnet despite all kinds of misconfigurations (T connectors with three
> leads, that kind of thing), whereas LAT would often show signs of
> trouble as soon as things weren't quite right, e.g. terminator omitted.

Well, the retransmitted messages are occurances that have happened over 
weeks earlier, communicating with normal DECservers. They are definitely 
not related to this, and they are not an indication of any problems here.

It is the "invalid message" that is the problem.

