I'm moving to faster and bigger disks on my hobbyist cluster, and also
to exclusively SBB disks; I have enough for the immediate future, but I
will need some additional SBB disks for spares as well as bigger (and
perhaps faster) disks for the future. 

Now, the fastest boxes I have are BA356 (20 MB/s with wide disks) and
the fastest disks are Ultra (40 MB/s on a wide bus).  Most of these are
4.3 GB but some are also 9.1 GB.  So I'm looking for disks in these
sizes as well as larger disks, preferably capable of at least 40 MB/s
(the minimum would be 20 MB/s, i.e. fast wide, though I would prefer
faster disks).  For now, they should at least work in a BA356, even
though that might reduce their speed. 

Now that I have faster SCSI controllers, I would like some SBB shelves
which are capable of at least 40 MB/s.  As a transition, one with the
same size slots as the BA356 would be practical (I'm assuming that
slower disks will work in the faster shelf).

Of course, everything should work with VMS.

What is the fastest shelf with the same slot size as the BA356?  What 
are the largest SBB disks at that speed?  At slower speeds?

I don't have a huge number of (logical) disks on my hobbyist cluster, 
but when one takes into account a) that all are shadowed (2 or sometimes 
3 members), 2) that I like to have enough spares to last me even after 
having replaced some disks and 3) that all boot servers have their own 
system disk (shadow set), the number of physical disks becomes quite 
large.  Also, if I move to faster and/or bigger disks, then these 
replace disks now in use, increasing the total number I need.

It would be nice to have some disks larger than 9.1 GB.  I would like to 
avoid volume sets as a way to increase the size of a logical disk 
(mainly since it is not trivial to transfer it later to a shadow set 
consisting of individual members).  (It's been a long time since I have 
done this.  Is my memory correct that I can't do this online, but rather 
have to dismount the disk which I want to expand (although I could at 
first create a volume set with just one member and later add to it, as 
one can also do with a shadow set)?  If so, this would make it even more 
difficult since the main disk I would need to expand can't be dismounted 
except by shutting down the entire cluster.)  So, in the second decade 
of the 21st century, please help me avoid volume sets!

I'm willing to collect; how far I'm willing to travel to do so depends 
on what is on offer.  Paying for shipping would be an option if the 
value (to me) of the shipment is large enough.  Although in special 
cases I might be willing to pay a "reasonable" fee, I am mainly looking 
to collect stuff (we are talking 1990s technology) which is no longer 
needed elsewhere.  Another option would be to trade some of my older 
stuff.

Please send any concrete requests or offers by email (spam block should 
be easy enough to decipher).

I am near Frankfurt am Main in Germany.

(I have now amassed almost all the hardware I need, at least stuff which 
has a street value near 0.  Other than the disks and shelves mentioned 
above, I would be looking only to slightly upgrade my existing stuff 
without any radical changes.  As such, workstations of the XP1000/DS10 
class (or better) and VGA LCD monitors are always welcome.)



