On 1/20/2011 11:06 PM, Adam Stouffer wrote:

> Looks like a touchy subject here. The truth is I collect old computers
> and once had a uVAX II but ended up scrapping it. The Alpha is no
> longer being used so I thought someone out there might have the same
> feeling about their VAX. I'm really just looking for a big old VAX
> with a scsi bus to toy around with.

The big old Vaxen did not have SCSI as a default option, and I am not 
sure if it was ever available for the Unibus or BI-Bus VAXen.

Vaxen with SCSI buses built in:

     VAX 4000-50 - 20 Vups  - It may be possible to power up the CPU 
module with out a q-bus and just run it.


     VAX 4000-1xx - 24 Vups and upward.

     VaxStation 31xx/4xxx - 3 Vups and up.

For VAXen 3xxx and 4xxx series you could add a KZQSA card for limited 
bootable SCSI support.  For real SCSI disk support you needed a third 
party card, and then it made the SCSI emulate q-bus controllers.

Since you want the actual hardware, I suppose that SimH VAX is not an 
option?

> Speaking of Ebay prices whats the deal with them anyhow? Are people
> just gouging because they figure you REALLY need the part? Some of the
> systems on there have a buy it now price higher than their original
> sale price!

You are competing with industrial users.  Some factory managers will 
avoid making a change in a process until they can not get replacement 
parts at any price, even though they could easily and cheaply substitute 
a more modern system.

They do not want to take the time to qualify the change, or risk that 
while it passed qualification, it might make a change that could cause a 
production outage or a recall.

Some processes require an expensive government or third party 
qualification for the slightest change.  I saw a report of over 
$1,000,000 U.S.D. cost for an aerospace component manufacturer.

The seller's of VAX and used VMS equipment know this very well.

-John
