In comp.sys.dec Adam Stouffer <adam_stouffer@hotmail.com> wrote:
>
> Looks like a touchy subject here. The truth is I collect old computers
> and once had a uVAX II but ended up scrapping it. The Alpha is no
> longer being used so I thought someone out there might have the same
> feeling about their VAX. I'm really just looking for a big old VAX
> with a scsi bus to toy around with.

The big old ones generally don't have SCSI.
since the SCSI add-on cards are very rare and expensive, most people
start collecting spare parts at some point and live with the old
and small disks. If you are a hobbyist, this isn't so bad, if
you still have a working machine.
I have a MicroVAX II with a 300MB ESDI disk. That's good enough.
You can do fine with even less disk space.
 
> Speaking of Ebay prices whats the deal with them anyhow? Are people
> just gouging because they figure you REALLY need the part? Some of the
> systems on there have a buy it now price higher than their original
> sale price!

there are still professional users, but also fans who are willing
to pay a lot to revive some memories. When you look at ebay, you
will often find some very expensive items which are never sold.
Those prices aren't for real. There are just some seller who think
they are sitting on pure gold, just because it says "DEC" on the
front. You should look at the completed auctions to get a feeling
for real prices. That may still be too much for you (or any other
hobbyist).

Dennis

