lets see, lots of places to start. First of all, lets make sure of the
identification and setup of the two boards you will need for a minimum
system:
M8186 CPU - KDF11-AA - How is it configured with the jumpers?  I would
suggest PowerUp Mode 1, which will immediately bring up ODT.  (W5-6),
W7 is OUT to allow entering of console ODT on halt, W1-master clock is
IN, W2 - factory installed-dont remove, W4-Event enable line is IN
(disabling bus event- may want to enable it later).

M8047-?? MXV11  Check configuration the -AA has 8K RAM, the CA has 32K
RAM.  I would set the board at factory defaults.
This ref may help you: http://www.fpns.net/willy/pdp11/BOARDS.HTM

Also, if you have not already done it, go to www.bitsavers.org and
look under digital directory (or maybe it is DEC, don't recall)
for .pdf versions of the board manuals, they are invaluable.

Once you have verified the jumper settings on both boards, I'd try
very simple operations:
1) Check the registers: store values in R1, R2, add them, verify the
the results by single stepping in ODT.  Do similar operations with R3,
R4, R5.
2) Try similar operations on memory, beginning at 1000.  Load A5 hex
into 1000 through 1020 and examine all the locations, then load 5A hex
and examine all locations.

If these operations turn out correct, the the CPU operation by itself
and the CPU+memory combination seem to work (at least over our simple
test set).

3) write a simple program, beginning at 1000, to increment a memory
location, perhaps 10 (decimal) times, run it, examine the memory
location to see if it is correct.

4) write a simple program to load a  memory location with 10 decimal,
decrement it, BEQ to zero to another location (perhaps 1020) which
contains a halt.

If all this works OK, then you can try talking to the serial port on
the MXV11.  You program above seems correct. I'd double check the base
address setting of the MXV11 and make sure the CSR and data buffer
registers in your program are correct for this board.  I believe they
are but I haven't done this in a looong time and memory is a little
rusty.

Good luck.
John


