I thought the US Government told everyone that MIL-STD-1750 processors
were the way forward? (mind you, was that back when they thought
16bits might be enough?)

Many issues DEC had in the commercial market, at least from where I
sat, were centred around most of DEC management's refusal to
acknowledge (at least until far too late) that cheaper alternatives
than VAX were becoming available, that could apparently do much of the
job for substantially less cost. This did eventually get fixed at
corporate level - by the time the PCI Alphas came out, both hardware
and software could be had for sensible prices, especially in
comparison with other UNIX boxes and almost in comparison with decent
NT/x86 boxes. But by then it was too late (especially with Palmer in
charge).

I'm not sure the cost of fabrication of VAX chips was ever that great
an issue, of itself. The minicomputer mindset left over from "VAX is
the answer. Now what's the question?" might well have been though.

At country level, for far too long, the message to the direct sales
force was still "keep the average system sales value up" rather than
"go out there and sell lots more systems, including sales to people
we've not seen before". The sales force (including management and
marketing) had largely got used to just waiting for orders to come in
from long standing customers.

Actively going out and seeking new sales opportunities wasn't a widely
understood activity; seeking new opportunities was (mistakenly)
believed to be something "the channel" (ie the resellers) would do
(which didn't happen very often in my experience). The few Sun
customers I knew who had Alpha/UNIX eval systems were generally well
impressed by price and performance, and a typical comment was "why has
no one told me about this before?". The NT/Alpha prospects I dealt
with in specific sectors where price/performance was critical liked
what they saw, apart from the lack of widespread application support
(and that was as much down to Gates as to anyone else).

Oh well.


