> private datacenter but if it is known to be broken it might save me from
> beating my head against the wall thnking I am doing something wrong.

I have seen two problems with the Linux LAT implementation.

1) It breaks the LAT protocol spec somewhere, somehow. This might be 
hard to spot, but I can tell on an RSX system, because the LAT process 
there have a counter for unrecognized frames in LAT, which slowly runs 
up when connected to from the Linux LAT (using llogin). What is worse 
(and this is not the Linux LAT fault) is that the RSX LAT code have a 
bug here. The buffer space allocated to such packets are never released 
again, so if someone use llogin to an RSX system, you will eventually 
run out of pool space on the RSX system, and you'll have to reboot.

2) At odd times, the Linux LAT seems to get out of sync. This might be 
related to 1) above, but I don't know. However, using a DECserver to 
connect to a Linux box, the session sometimes hang (it might take a 
while for it to happen), and I need to disconnect and reconnect again.

The problems are not bad enough to make it unusable, but they are bad 
enough that I'm constantly thinking of writing my own. The specs for LAT 
are out there, so it just needs some time (which I have way too little).

	Johnny

