> You could certainly see where the inspiration might have come from,
> but it was far too late to market to stand any real chance of
> competing with x86, 68K, and the like. And soon after that, RISC
> became trendy. Now, with the benefit of hindsight, we know RISC is
> neither here nor there, maybe just a distraction really, but back then
> no one expected a CISC chip such as x86 to achieve the kind of
> performance AMD and Intel have achieved.

Um, humbly beg to differ with the phrase "RISC is neither here nor
there, maybe just a distraction really,"  I consider it more of an
enlightenment.

It became a forcing function for architecture design.  as the x86
evolved, 486, pentium, Pentium pro adopted a RISC philosophy in its
instruction sets...the problem was they needed to maintain backward
compatibility (at least it was seen as an important factor). As Intel
evolved the architecture, I remember getting mailings about using
'core' set of instructions which had smaller instruction execution
times then some of their others.  While I agree the base hw
implementation was CISC than RISC, it was a slight of hand to allow
more favorable overall program execution time compared to the pure
RISC machines at the time.  As the intel architecture has evolved,
they became more RISC like (more execution units, and simple(r)
instructions to take advantage of them.).

The evolution of multicore processors have embraced RISC architecture
for a number of reasons: 'simplicity' of execution units to minimize
heating, simple an efficient routing of 1 and 2 level caches for
multiple processors, simpler comparisons of processor state where
synchronization is important.

J


