> Maybe the Linux version of the protocol itself is not greatly
> different, but supposing there is a minor incompatibility or bug
> (maybe due to reverse engineering rather than having access to specs)
> which results in a resource leak in some circumstances in some
> implementations. On a DECserver 90 you have a 32bit processor with
> maybe megabytes of RAM. If it's a leaking a few bytes per session,
> maybe it will be a while before anyone notice. If we're talking a few
> bytes per session of lost pool on an RSX box, how long before that
> starts being visible? [This is an extension of Johnny Billquist's idea
> from 3:56 on Monday]

Yes, and no.

The memory leak only happens when you use the Linux LAT (llogin) to RSX 
systems. It does not happen for any DECserver. Nor does it (as far as I 
know) happen if you connect from a VMS system to RSX via LAT.

The memory leak happens in chunks, when a bad packet is received from 
the Linux LAT by RSX.

As for the lost connections in the other scenario I mentioned, that have 
happened for me using a DECserver-300 connecting to a Linux system. The 
session can work for sometimes just a few minutes, at other times for 
hours. So I do not know what exact circumstance triggers that bug. But 
what happens is that output stops coming from the Linux system, and at 
that point I cannot get any more data out. I need to disconnect on the 
DECserver, and open a new session to the Linux system.

	Johnny

