Having more disks helps but more than one operating system could sit
on the same disk.
aboot is capable of booting at least the NetBSD kernel, but I haven't
tryied for a real installation, only for test.

> I have previously run NetBSD quite successfully on an old dell laptop.
> Obviously the architecture is different but of course at an operating
> system level everything would be fairly familiar.

GNU userland is different from BSD userland, same commands but
sligthly different behaviuor.
Anyway, you could always install the GNU tools from PKGSRC.

> Is it the case that there will continue to be updates to Debian/Alpha,
> even if an unofficial capacity?

Well, Alpha isn't a target architecture for releases (as it is not
m68k for example).
So, there will never be an official Debian 6.0, but the development
goes on and packages continue to be built.
We will always have the SID (development) branch for Debian so in mid
term the support will be good.
I doubt we will see OpenOffice and Chromium on Alpha, but yet there is
a fully working OpenJDK port for Debian Alpha.

Enjoy!
gl


