Bob Eager <rde42@spamcop.net> spake the secret code
<8ohkmhFrc7U6@mid.individual.net> thusly:

>But this book may help...even the reviews say something...
>
> http://www.amazon.com/DEC-Dead-Long-Live-Corporation/dp/1576752259

I read this book when it came out.  I liked it and would recommend it.
If someone else here reads it and would like to talk more about the
book, I'll get my copy out and reskim it.

The author interviewed many of the principals involved and didn't conclude
that there was any single factor that caused the demise of DEC.  I guess
technically DEC didn't "die" like SGI (bankruptcy followed by fire-sale
purchase of assets), but was acquired by Compaq, although by then it
was much smaller then the DEC of its prime.

IIRC, while each principal interviewed felt they knew one key factor that
caused the demise of DEC, the author found that many of the different
arguments had merit.

Personally it felt to me like DEC never really transitioned from minis
to workstations.  The transition from workstations to PCs was even
more difficult.  When people were transitioning from shared machines
to workstations (say mid 80s to mid 90s), DEC seemed to be pursuing a
more "mainframey" kind of business.  This seems ironic when you
consider that what made DEC successful with the mini was going away
from the centralized big machine into a more personable smaller
machine.  Maybe if they had continued that trend and worked on making
ever smaller and ever more personable machines they would have
survived the transition.

Lots of other companies have had difficulty surviving a major
technology change like this.  SGI never really transitioned into the
PC world, become more and more of a niche player until all its talent
and market had been ceded to PC graphics companies like NVidia and ATI.

Evans & Sutherland's design systems groups never successfully made the
transition from minicomputer based stuff to workstations.  By the time
they entered the workstation market in 1989 (I worked on that
product), it was too late to enter the market and be a significant
player.  (A story I heard was that Dave Evans was approached by Jim
Clark to make workstations and Evans is reputed to have stated that
the mainframe would always be king.  In response Clark went to
Stanford and started working on what would become the core tech of
SGI.)  E&S is a company of approximately 60 employees, having either
laid off or sold off most of its business units.  They sold off their
flight simulator group to Rockwell-Collins in 2006.  All they have
left under the E&S name is a digital projector product and the
planetarium products.

I've heard that Bill Gates would spend most of his "strategy" thinking
on what would be the next wave of disruptive technology that would
catch Microsoft off-guard.  One could make an argument that the
explosion of the internet and web browsers in the mid 90s almost
caught MS with their pants down until they did a full-court press and
made a reasonable competitor with IE4.  Novell never did seem to quite
"get it" with regards to the internet and has yet to recapture its glory 
days as a networking player.  With Xbox and Windows Phone 7, MS is
managing to stay relevant in spaces where it can't get there with
Windows desktops.
