Not sure who 'we' referres to.  I'll throw in my $0.02....Being
primarily a hardware engineer people would have gotten a vastly more
reliable hardware platform than the IBM PC (as well as the SGI and
similar platforms). They also would have gotten a better architecture
CPU in the KDJ11-A CPU that would support multitasking OSs (e.g.
rsx-11), run at 15 or 18 MHZ, Qbus had much better noise immunity and
speed than the various PC buses.  The microchannel and EISA buses were
the only ones that came somewhat close to the characteristics of the
Qbus.  Why does all this matter? Many of these 16bit platforms were
put in *many* industrial control situations where they 'just kept
working'...I constantly fought battles wrt putting in a PC instead of
an 11/23 or 11/73 board based system.  Lets just say one 'industrial
PC' experience taught the company a $2M lesson when PCs controlling a
process died and caused major havoc.  DECs share of computing
platforms in the glass, steel, aluminum, and news paper industries
took large hits.
On the software side, you would have gotten a real multitasking kernel
with hardware architecture support.  I still believe that M$ set
computing back 15 years by announcing in 1993 that Windoz
'multiasking' was so great that now you could send a job to the
printer and not have to wait.  Hmmmm, gee, I was doing this in
undergrad in the early 70s under RSX....
Also at the time we were delivering some applications with Unix on the
DEC platform (a hard sell to the process control industry in the late
80s and early 90s).
If we needed high res and super graphics support for an application,
we often speced SGI platforms as front ends to the process control
machines as well as microvaxes but lost the $ war because the MVaxes-
even the GS version were at a deficit when it came to high performance
graphics/$.
I also remember fighting development cost battles with my management
at the time about software tools - a base Fortran  compiler from DEC
was $20K and fairly good Fortran and C compilers could be had for the
PCs for less than 3K.  It was also very difficult to pass any of the
development tool cost to the customer, or if I tried, they would ask
the same justification questions as my management, so I'd have to
fight battles on two fronts.

Anyway, to just another PC reseller I would add: a reliable hardware
platform, superior architecture in both the KDJ11 and the Alpha, and a
better OS (either RT or RSX) than DOS, with the native ability to
support UNIX.
J


