In 1989 I had a contract at Chase bank developing a system to
price off-the-run treasuries for that trading desk, under vax/vms.
During acceptance, I often visited traders there. A few desks
away, all those traders were having their vms terminals replaced by
Apollo unix workstations. Didn't escape my notice.
  Then in 1992 when Olsen gave way to Palmer, I knew the
writing was on the wall. And when Palmer started selling off
pieces, like Rdb, the future was like a crystal ball.
  When Compaq finally acquired Digital, I had a vms contract at
Bankers Trust. The morning when the news broke, I came into
work and half the staff looked to me like they were in utter
shock. I'd personally realized this day was coming for quite
some time, and couldn't understand the shock/disbelief/whatever
expressed by so many (although maybe a third felt just
like I did). It was pretty much a grief response, from people
who'd built a career on vms, now realizing that future was gone.
I guess it's hard needing to rewrite a high-salaried resume
boasting "senior vms...15 years experience" to one timidly
saying "unix novice", or words to those effects.
