Back around the mid 80's to early 90's you are correct.
By the time the 68020 and 386 arrived the chance to beat those chips
and architectures in the 32 bit area was dead and the 16 bit one
was pretty much dying.

The J11 was too limited.  It was obsolete with limited memory
addressing, limited production runs, no future speed bumps beyond 18mhz.

The 68k would put it in the grave shortly...

But, if DEC had gone the way they should have... DEC could've OEM'd the
MicroVax chipset and had serious production with a second source.

The combination of VAX/VMS, Unix, 32 bit virtual memory and a known
architecture would've made them a winner if they could've had the
production going.

At the time the military had RCA, GE, Raytheon and a bunch of other
vendors working on a MCF Vax-a-like 32 bit chip.  The 68k and MicroVax
pretty much killed the project.  The pentagon told the vendors they were
going to go Commercial off the shelf (COTS).

DEC could've been the Intel of the 90's if they didn't have the
minicomputer cost structure and if they could have had an ability
to fabricate uVaxII's as fast as Intel and others did the 80286.

Perhaps a production deal with IBM or Motorola or even Intel might have
helped.

DEC had the CPU architecture but not any idea on volume production and
sales.  In their mind they were in the "minicomputer" business not the computer
business. 

